---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import LeadForm from "../components/LeadForm.astro";
import CallLink from "../components/CallLink.astro";

const title = "Water damage restoration in Suwanee, GA";
const description =
  "Burst pipe, leak, or creek water in Suwanee, GA 30024. Call or send what happened.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="hero wrap">
    <p class="muted">Gwinnett County</p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Water where it doesn't belong? We're here to help. Call or click "Request
      help in Suwanee" now and fill out the form. Share as much as you can about
      the problem and we will get back to you as soon as we can.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href="#request">Request help in Suwanee</a>
      <CallLink class="btn secondary" />
      <a class="btn secondary" href="/what-to-do">First-hour checklist</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Water damage in Suwanee</h2>
    <p>
      The railroad tracks split Suwanee in two. On one side is Town Center, with
      the park, the fountain, the amphitheater, and City Hall. On the other is
      Old Town, with Main Street and the red caboose. Freight trains run through
      both, and people here navigate by which side of the tracks they are on.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most of the houses outside Old Town were built from the mid-1990s on.
      Morningview was built around 1996 to 2000. Town Center and Shadowbrook came
      in the mid-2000s. So many homes are 20 to 30 years old. That is the age
      when the water heater, the washer hose, and the refrigerator line start to
      fail. The usual call is a leak, not a flood.
    </p>
    <p>
      Old Town is different. It has older houses on small lots. Many are still on
      septic, not sewer. The city says there is an easy way to tell. Look at your
      Gwinnett County water bill. If you pay nothing for sewer, you are probably
      on septic.
    </p>
    <p>
      What is under the house matters. Many houses in Morningview have basements,
      and some are daylight basements. Older houses in Old Town more often sit
      over a crawl space. On a slab, water spreads across the floor. Tell us what
      you have, and if you are not sure, say so.
    </p>
    <p>
      Suwanee also has a creek. During Hurricane Helene in 2024, Suwanee Creek
      Park, Martin Farm Park, George Pierce Park, the greenway, and the
      playground behind Suwanee Elementary all flooded. If you live near the
      creek, that is not just a park problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is why the first questions are about your house. When you call or send
      the form, tell us where the water came from, which rooms are wet, and how
      long it has been there. If you are on septic, say so.
    </p>
    <p>
      Storms do some damage. In a January 2022 storm, trees came down in Suwanee.
      A damaged roof can leak into the attic for days before the stain shows up
      on the ceiling.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Where people say they live</h2>
    <p>
      Suwanee has one ZIP code, 30024. Some addresses with a Suwanee mailing
      address are actually in Forsyth County, not Suwanee.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Town Center and Shadowbrook.</strong> Mid-2000s houses and
      three-level townhomes. People walk to the park, and the lots are small.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Old Town.</strong> Older and historic houses on small lots. Many are
      on septic.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Morningview.</strong> Brick houses from about 1996 to 2000. Many
      have basements.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Laurel Springs.</strong> A gated golf neighborhood from the 1990s.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Grand Cascades and Olde Atlanta Club.</strong> Larger houses from
      the late 1990s and 2000s.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The River Club.</strong> Custom homes from the 2000s, on the
      Chattahoochee.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Suwanee Station and Rivermoore Park.</strong> Houses from the 2000s,
      close to I-85.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Maple Ridge.</strong> Ranch and two-story houses from the 1980s.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Common causes in Suwanee</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Water heater.</strong> The tank leaks or lets go in a garage or closet.
        This is common in houses from the late 1990s and 2000s.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Refrigerator or washer line.</strong> The hose cracks. Water slides
        under the floor, and you smell it before you see it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Creek water.</strong> After heavy rain, the creek can rise into
        yards and low parts of the house near the greenway.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Septic backup.</strong> In Old Town, wastewater can back up into a
        tub or floor drain. This is sewage.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Burst pipe.</strong> After a hard freeze, a pipe can split in a wall
        or an attic. Gwinnett had a lot of these in December 2022.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Storm damage.</strong> Rain gets in through a window, a door, or a
        roof damaged by wind or a falling tree.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets.</strong> Do not clean these
        up yourself. Call.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>If water is in the house now</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Shut the water off, if you can do it safely. Stay away from wet outlets.</li>
      <li>Take photos of the water, the room, and where it started.</li>
      <li>
        Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets: <CallLink prefix="call" /> or
        <a href="#request">send the form</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Already stopped a small drip? Use the
        <a href="/small-leaks">small-leak steps</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Call your insurance company when you can. If you plan to file a claim, do
      not start cutting out drywall first.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not wait until tomorrow to dry things out. Wet drywall and flooring get
      worse overnight, and mold can start in 24 to 48 hours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Questions from Suwanee homeowners</h2>
    <dl class="faq">
      <div>
        <dt>How do I know if my house is on septic or sewer?</dt>
        <dd>
          Look at your Gwinnett County water bill. If it has no sewer charge, the
          house is probably on septic. This matters when you call, because a
          septic backup is a different problem from a sewer backup. Tell us which
          you have, or tell us you are not sure.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Is there a way to tell if the creek is going to reach my house?</dt>
        <dd>
          Suwanee Creek has a gauge, and the National Weather Service posts
          forecasts for it. Flood stage is 8 feet. At 11 feet, the greenway has 1
          to 3 feet of water on it, and some yards along the creek take water.
          Check the gauge when heavy rain is coming, and move anything valuable up
          off the floor. We cannot tell you about your own house. Ask your insurer
          about outside water coverage before the storm, not after.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          My address says Suwanee but I think I am in Forsyth County. Does that
          matter?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          It might. Some addresses with a Suwanee mailing address are in Forsyth
          County, not Gwinnett. When you call or send the form, give your street
          and ZIP, and say if you think you are in Forsyth. That way the right
          company can answer.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>I live in a gated neighborhood. How does a crew get in?</dt>
        <dd>
          Tell us the gate code or the guard house name when you call, or put it
          in the form. If the gate needs a name on the list, put the name on the
          list before the crew comes. If you are not home, ask a neighbor to meet
          them.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Questions about drying time, insurance, or whether you have to hire the
      company that calls are answered on <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> and
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <p>
      <strong>What a job looks like.</strong> A crew checks the house, pulls out
      standing water, dries the structure, and replaces what can't be saved. You
      should be shown moisture readings, not just told it looks dry. How long it
      takes depends on the job. <a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Clean water, dirty water, sewage.</strong> A broken supply line is
      not the same as a washer overflow or a sewer or septic backup. Say which one
      it is on the form.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Insurance.</strong> A sudden burst pipe or a failed appliance is often
      covered. A slow leak, outside water, or sewage may not be unless you bought extra
      coverage. Take photos first. Don't tear out drywall to "help." Ask your insurer.
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;" id="request">
    <h2>Request help</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Name, phone, city, ZIP, and what happened. Someone will call you back.
    </p>
    <LeadForm defaultCity="suwanee" />
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h2>Nearby</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/buford">Buford</a> ·
      <a href="/duluth">Duluth</a> ·
      <a href="/hamilton-mill">Hamilton Mill</a> ·
      <a href="/lawrenceville">Lawrenceville</a> ·
      <a href="/gwinnett-county">Gwinnett County</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
